Flush stdio buffers before starting a child process.
spawn() on other platforms calls fflush_all() before it forks, so that
output buffered by the parent comes out before the child's. The Windows
spawn functions did not, so when stdout was a pipe or a file, output
such as that of find -print stayed in the buffer while the commands run
by -exec wrote theirs, and came out after it.

Flush all streams in mingw_spawnvp(), which the spawn functions share.

---
 win32/process.c |    4 ++++
 1 file changed, 4 insertions(+)

--- a/win32/process.c
+++ b/win32/process.c
@@ -355,6 +355,10 @@
 	char *path;
 	intptr_t ret;
 
+	/* Like spawn() on other platforms: don't let buffered output
+	 * appear after the child's */
+	fflush_all();
+
 #if ENABLE_FEATURE_PREFER_APPLETS && NUM_APPLETS > 1
 	if ((!has_path(cmd) || unix_path(cmd)) &&
 			find_applet_by_name(bb_basename(cmd)) >= 0)
